trace.content:
[THOUGHT] The absence of systemic or meningeal symptoms (no fever, headache, neck stiffness, etc.) makes acute opportunistic meningitis or encephalitis less likely and further supports PML as the leading diagnosis — a subacute, non-inflammatory, demyelinating process caused by JC virus reactivation in immunosuppression. However, to confirm localization and severity of neurological involvement — and to distinguish PML from other white matter disorders (e.g., HIV-associated vacuolar myelopathy affecting spinal cord, or stroke) — I need objective physical exam findings. Specifically: Is the right leg weakness consistent with an upper motor neuron pattern (e.g., spasticity, hyperreflexia, Babinski sign)? Is there associated sensory loss, ataxia, or visual field cut? Gait assessment (e.g., hemiparetic vs. ataxic vs. myelopathic) will help localize (cortical, subcortical, brainstem, spinal cord). This is domain #7 (targeted physical examination), still uncovered — and essential before concluding.

[TEST] Comprehensive neurological examination including:  
- Right leg strength (graded 0–5), tone, deep tendon reflexes (patellar, Achilles), and Babinski sign;  
- Gait assessment (tandem, heel-to-toe, observation for hemiparesis/ataxia);  
- Visual fields to confrontation;  
- Cerebellar testing (finger-nose, heel-shin);  
- Mental status screening (orientation, recall, attention, executive function).

